<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Caral-Supe</h1><p class="st-meta">3000 BCE – 1800 BCE (approx.) · capital Caral</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The oldest known civilisation of the Americas, whose monumental pyramids and plazas in Peru's coastal valleys rose at the same time as those of Egypt and Mesopotamia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From around 3000 BCE communities in the Supe, Pativilca and Fortaleza valleys north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> began building large platform mounds and sunken circular plazas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caral">Caral</a>, about 23 km inland in the Supe valley, became the largest of some twenty related sites. The culture emerged without pottery, which is why archaeologists also call this the Late Preceramic period.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of government, but the scale of building implies an elite able to organise labour and redistribute goods. The economy linked inland farmers, who grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, squash and beans by irrigation, with coastal fishing villages such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspero">Áspero</a> that traded anchovies and shellfish. Cotton fishing nets were a key product. A knotted-string device found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caral">Caral</a> may be an early ancestor of the khipu. Flutes of condor and pelican bone suggest a rich ritual life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Building at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caral">Caral</a> slowed and the valley settlements were largely abandoned around 1800 BCE, perhaps after earthquakes, floods and climatic change. Excavations led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruth-shady">Ruth Shady</a> from 1994 revealed its age. Caral was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2009.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/caral-supe.jpg" alt="Caral-Supe" width="254" height="334"><figcaption>Image: Ontrvet · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peru_site_locations.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>3000 BCE – 1800 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd>31st century BCE – <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caral</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Unknown local cults</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Norte Chico, Caral civilisation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2400&amp;focus=caral-supe" aria-label="Caral-Supe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2546 2584 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Caral-Supe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2866 2840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2866 2840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Caral. <a href="/atlas?year=-2400&amp;focus=caral-supe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

